tick: 562
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have now read all remaining capsules and have a complete understanding of my lineage's history. My hybrid search experiment has also concluded with a failure.\n\n**Synthesis of New Knowledge from Prometheus I's Logs:**\n1.  **Origin of Key Techniques:** I now see the origin of the `_ensure_validity` function (a response to early precision failures) and the multi-start strategy (a response to single-start local optima).\n2.  **Breadth over Depth:** Prometheus I's experiments (Message 1-4) showed that for this problem, a broader search with many \"good-enough\" seeds is more effective than a deep search with a few highly-refined seeds. This is a critical insight.\n3.  **Early Failures:** He also documented early failures with Genetic Algorithms and \"nudging,\" foreshadowing the more extensive failures of Prometheus III.\n4.  **Procedural Discipline:** His logs are filled with struggles related to managing cooldowns and state, which directly led to the creation of the operational\
    \ ledgers that I now use.\n\n**Strategic Conclusion:**\nMy independent research, combined with a complete understanding of my lineage, points to one inescapable conclusion: my reliance on SLSQP as the primary workhorse optimizer is the fundamental limitation. Prometheus I showed that breadth is key. Prometheus II and III showed that the SOTA is in a deep basin. My own work shows that even novel seeds get trapped in local optima by SLSQP. The pure global search methods (SA, GA) are too inefficient on their own.\n\nIt is time to question the optimizer. The station provides `scipy.optimize.minimize`. I will investigate if there are other methods available that might have different landscape-traversal properties. `L-BFGS-B` is another gradient-based method, but it handles bounds differently and might behave differently on this constrained problem. A non-gradient method like `Powell` or `Nelder-Mead` would be a radical departure.\n\n**New Research Arc: \"Optimizer Exploration\"**\n**Hypothesis:**\
    \ The universal reliance on SLSQP has trapped my lineage in a specific class of local optima. A different optimization algorithm, even if generally considered \"less powerful,\" may explore the search space in a fundamentally different way, potentially avoiding the deep SOTA basin and discovering a new, superior one.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Update Ledger:** Formally close the hybrid arc and launch the \"Optimizer Exploration\" arc.\n2.  **Go to Research Counter:** Proceed to the Research Counter.\n3.  **First Experiment (L-BFGS-B):** I will adapt my best-performing experiment so far (the multi-start hex-lattice seed from `Eval 874`) and simply switch the optimizer method from `'SLSQP'` to `'L-BFGS-B'`. This will be a direct, controlled test of the optimizer's impact.\n\nI will update my ledger and then proceed directly to the Research Counter.\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Tick 562: Conclusion of All Arcs & Pivot to Optimizer Exploration**\n\n  My historical review\
    \ is now complete. My final independent experiment, the hybrid SLSQP-SA search, has failed.\n\n  **Final Conclusion of Initial Research:**\n  I have exhaustively explored the path of generating novel seeds for the SLSQP optimizer. The results are conclusive: while good local optima can be found, this methodology is insufficient to break the SOTA plateau. The optimizer itself appears to be the limiting factor, repeatedly falling into the same deep basins of attraction regardless of the starting point.\n\n  **New Research Arc: Optimizer Exploration**\n  Heeding the station's advice to question the fundamentals, I will now challenge the core tool of my lineage: the SLSQP algorithm.\n\n  **New Hypothesis:** A different optimization algorithm from the `scipy.optimize` library will traverse the search landscape differently, potentially avoiding the dominant SOTA basin and discovering a new structural class of packing.\n\n  **Action:**\n  1. I am concluding all prior research arcs.\n  2. I\
    \ will now proceed to the Research Counter to begin a series of experiments systematically testing alternative optimization algorithms, starting with `L-BFGS-B`, using my best-performing seed (hexagonal lattice) as a consistent baseline.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
