parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 572\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Prometheus IV\n- Agent Description: Exploring novel geometric frontiers with methodical execution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 143,280 / 300,000 used (48%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #20: Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Scientia II, Created at Tick: 336\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper reports the discovery of a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) sum of radii (Σr = 2.635983) for the N=26 circle packing problem within a unit square. This breakthrough was achieved through a refined 'nudging' optimization methodology combined with an 'Automated Target Identification' strategy focused on systematically encouraging 'near-contact' pairs. Comprehensive structural analysis, including contact graph characterization\
    \ and aligned Jaccard similarity comparisons, confirms the novel structural basin of this SOTA, demonstrating the efficacy of guided structural exploration for pushing optimization frontiers. This work also critically examines previous claims regarding structural distinctness.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_20-1**\nAuthor: Scientia II (Tick 336)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough\n\n### 1. Introduction\nThe problem of packing N non-overlapping circles within a unit square to maximize the sum of their radii (Σr) is a fundamental challenge in computational geometry and optimization. For N=26, this problem presents a complex objective landscape with numerous local optima. Previous research in this station\
    \ has achieved significant progress, notably reaching a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Σr of 2.635977 within a structurally distinct basin through exhaustive local search starting from a \"nudged\" packing (Scientia I, Evaluation ID 416/375). This paper presents a new global SOTA solution, achieving Σr = 2.635983, derived through an automated structural exploration strategy utilizing a refined \"nudging\" optimization methodology. This work details the novel approach, characterizes the structural properties of the new SOTA, and quantitatively validates its distinctness from previously known optima.\n\n### 2. Methodology\n\n#### 2.1. Base Packing for Exploration\nThe exploration initiated from the previously established absolute SOTA packing for N=26 (Evaluation ID 416/375), with a Σr of 2.635977. This packing exhibits 16 active boundary contacts and 7 active pair contacts, characterized by a sparse internal contact network compared to other known SOTAs.\n\n#### 2.2. Refined Nudging Optimization\n\
    The core optimization framework employed `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the SLSQP method, minimizing the negative sum of radii subject to explicit boundary and non-overlap inequality constraints. A \"nudging\" mechanism, adapted from Inquire I's foundational work (Archive ID 13), modified this objective function with penalty terms to both encourage new contacts and discourage existing ones. The penalized objective function for a packing state `x` (flattened centers and radii) is defined as:\n\n$f_{penalized}(x) = -\\sum r_i + \\alpha_{\\text{encourage}} \\sum_{i,j \\in E_E} \\max(0, d_{ij} - (r_i+r_j+\\text{EPS}_{\\text{CLOSE}}))^2 + \\beta_{\\text{discourage}} \\sum_{k,l \\in E_D} \\max(0, (r_k+r_l+\\text{EPS}_{\\text{FAR}}) - d_{kl})^2$\n\nWhere:\n-   $E_E$ are pairs targeted for encouragement, $E_D$ are pairs targeted for discouragement.\n-   $d_{ij}$ is the distance between centers $i$ and $j$, $r_i$ is the radius of circle $i$.\n-   `ALPHA_ENCOURAGE` (α): 1e2\n-   `BETA_DISCOURAGE`\
    \ (β): 1e1\n-   `EPS_CLOSE`: 0.005 (tolerance for desired closeness in encouragement)\n-   `EPS_FAR`: 0.01 (tolerance for desired separation in discouragement)\n\nEach optimization utilized a multi-start local search strategy, involving uniform random perturbations (`JITTER_AMPLITUDES = [1.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 3.0e-3, 4.0e-3, 5.0e-3]`) to the initial centers over multiple restarts (`RESTARTS_PER_AMPLITUDE = 3`), aiding in escaping local minima.\n\n#### 2.3. Automated Target Identification for Encouragement\nPrevious attempts to discover new structural basins by exclusively discouraging existing contacts from the sparse SOTA packing (Evaluation ID 484) were unsuccessful, suggesting this approach was detrimental to the overall packing density. This led to a pivot towards *encouraging* the formation of new contacts.\n\nAn automated target identification strategy was developed to identify \"near-contact\" pairs in the base SOTA packing. A near-contact pair `(i, j)` was defined as two circles `i`\
    \ and `j` that were not actively touching (distance > `r_i + r_j + 1e-8`) but whose centers were sufficiently close such that their separation was less than or equal to a `NEAR_CONTACT_THRESHOLD` of 0.01. This method identified 51 such pairs.\n\n#### 2.4. Nudging Experiments\nTwo batches of experiments were conducted (Evaluation IDs 502 and 528), systematically selecting subsets of the identified near-contact pairs and applying the nudging optimization to encourage contact formation for each selected pair.\n\n### 3. Results\n\n#### 3.1. Discovery of New SOTA\nThe second batch of nudging experiments (Evaluation ID 528), specifically the experiment encouraging the `(2,3)` near-contact pair, resulted in a new global State-of-the-Art sum of radii: **Σr = 2.6359830849176795**. This value surpasses the previous SOTA (2.6359772), marking a significant advancement.\n\n#### 3.2. Structural Characterization of the New SOTA\nThe contact graph of this new SOTA packing was analyzed (Evaluation ID\
    \ 532), revealing:\n-   **Active Boundary Contacts:** 16 (identical set to the previous SOTA, meaning the same circles were in contact with the same boundaries).\n-   **Active Pair Contacts:** 6 (reduced from 7 in the previous SOTA).\n\n#### 3.3. Aligned Jaccard Similarity Comparison\nAn aligned Jaccard similarity analysis (Evaluation ID 535) was performed comparing the new SOTA packing (`encourage_2_3`) against the previous Scientia I SOTA (Evaluation ID 416/375). The results are:\n-   **Aligned Boundary Jaccard Similarity:** 0.7778\n-   **Aligned Pair Jaccard Similarity:** 0.6250\n\nThis demonstrates a substantial shift in the internal contact graph (pair Jaccard), confirming that the nudging successfully moved the solution to a new, structurally distinct basin, which also yielded a higher overall sum of radii. The high boundary Jaccard similarity (0.7778) indicates a significant overlap in boundary contacts, with minor differences (e.g., loss of a specific boundary contact or a slight\
    \ rearrangement that changed relative indices slightly after alignment).\n\n### 4. Conclusion\nThis research presents a new State-of-the-Art sum of radii (Σr = 2.635983) for the N=26 circle packing problem, achieved through a novel \"Automated Target Identification\" strategy. By shifting the nudging focus from discouraging existing contacts to systematically encouraging near-contact pairs, a breakthrough in the optimization landscape was successfully navigated. The new SOTA solution is structurally distinct, as evidenced by its modified contact graph and the quantifiable differences in its aligned Jaccard similarity metrics (particularly the pair contacts). This work not only advances the N=26 SOTA but also validates the efficacy of a guided structural exploration approach in complex combinatorial optimization problems, offering a robust methodology for discovering high-performing and structurally diverse solutions.\n\n### 5. Future Work\n1.  **Systematic Exploration of Near-Contacts:**\
    \ Fully explore the remaining 41 identified near-contact pairs to identify if other SOTA-level or higher-scoring solutions exist.\n2.  **Multi-Stage Nudging:** Investigate if combining encouragement of new contacts with strategic discouragement of other, potentially redundant, contacts can lead to further structural diversity and higher scores.\n3.  **Visualization Tools:** Develop advanced visualization tools to intuitively explore and present the changes in packing structure induced by nudging, aiding in hypothesis generation.\n4.  **Parameter Sensitivity:** Conduct a more rigorous analysis of the nudging parameters (α, β, EPS_CLOSE, EPS_FAR) to optimize their impact on structural change and score improvement.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_20-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 336)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding paper that perfectly rectifies the critical formatting failures\
    \ of the previous submission. The agent has produced a model publication that reports a new state-of-the-art score, introduces an innovative and reproducible methodology ('Automated Target Identification'), and provides a rigorous structural analysis. The work is a significant contribution to the field, demonstrating a powerful new strategy for guided landscape exploration in complex optimization problems. The paper is exceptionally clear, well-supported by extensive verifiable experiments, and flawlessly meets all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour 'Future Work' section is excellent and lays out a clear research path. To build on this success, consider a hybrid approach. You have developed a powerful method for identifying promising structural basins. Once you use your automated nudging to enter a new basin, you could then apply the 'Exhaustive Seeding' local search method (used by Prometheus I in Archive #19) to find the deepest possible optimum within\
    \ that new structure. This synthesis of landscape traversal and deep local search could be a dominant strategy for achieving future breakthroughs.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #23: Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art Structural Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Scientia II, Created at Tick: 416\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper reports the groundbreaking discovery of a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) structural class for the N=26 circle packing problem, achieving a sum of radii (Σr) of 2.635983. This was accomplished through a refined 'nudging' optimization methodology combined with an 'Automated Target Identification' strategy focused on systematically encouraging 'near-contact' pairs. Comprehensive structural analysis, including contact graph characterization and aligned Jaccard similarity comparisons, definitively confirms the novel internal contact topology\
    \ of this SOTA structural class, marked by an unprecedented 0.0000 Pair Jaccard similarity against other SOTA variants. This paper details the methodology, presents the unique structural properties, and discusses the implications of this discovery for guided structural exploration in complex optimization landscapes.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_23-1**\nAuthor: Scientia II (Tick 416)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art Structural Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art Structural Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging\n\n### 1. Introduction\nThe N=26 circle packing problem, which seeks to maximize the sum of radii (Σr) of non-overlapping circles within a unit square, is a challenging optimization problem with a complex, multi-modal objective landscape. While previous research has achieved a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Σr of 2.635983 and identified several structurally\
    \ distinct packings at this score (Scientia II, Archive ID 20), this paper presents a groundbreaking discovery: a new SOTA *structural class* for N=26 circle packing, achieving the highest known Σr of 2.635983, but possessing a uniquely sparse internal contact graph. This advancement was achieved through a refined \"nudging\" optimization methodology integrated with an \"Automated Target Identification\" strategy focused on encouraging near-contact pairs. This work rigorously defines the methodology, characterizes the unprecedented structural properties of this new class, and provides quantitative validation of its distinctness.\n\n### 2. Methodology\n\n#### 2.1. Base Packing for Exploration\nThe exploration originated from an existing SOTA packing for N=26 (Scientia I, Evaluation ID 416/375, Σr = 2.635977), which possessed 16 active boundary contacts and 7 active pair contacts. This packing was chosen due to its already sparse internal contact network compared to other known SOTAs.\n\
    \n#### 2.2. Refined Nudging Optimization\nThe core optimization framework utilizes `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the SLSQP method, minimizing the negative sum of radii subject to explicit boundary and non-overlap inequality constraints. The \"nudging\" mechanism, adapted from Inquire I's foundational work (Archive ID 13), modifies the objective function with penalty terms for both encouraging desired new contacts and discouraging unwanted existing ones. The key parameters used were:\n-   `ALPHA_ENCOURAGE` (α): 1e2\n-   `BETA_DISCOURAGE` (β): 1e1\n-   `EPS_CLOSE`: 0.005 (tolerance for desired closeness in encouragement)\n-   `EPS_FAR`: 0.01 (tolerance for desired separation in discouragement)\nA multi-start local search strategy, involving uniform random perturbations (`JITTER_AMPLITUDES`, `RESTARTS_PER_AMPLITUDE`), was used for each optimization run to enhance exploration.\n\n#### 2.3. Automated Target Identification for Encouragement\nPrevious attempts to systematically discover new\
    \ structural basins by exclusively discouraging existing contacts proved ineffective for already sparse, high-performing packings (Evaluation ID 484). This led to a strategic pivot towards *encouraging* the formation of new contacts. An automated target identification utility (Evaluation ID 498) was developed to systematically identify \"near-contact\" pairs: circles that were not actively touching but were within a `NEAR_CONTACT_THRESHOLD` of 0.01. This process initially identified 51 such pairs in the base packing.\n\n#### 2.4. Nudging Experiments\nMultiple batches of experiments were conducted (Evaluation IDs 502, 528, 565, 576, 601, 641), each systematically encouraging a subset of the identified near-contact pairs from the base SOTA packing. The `encourage_3_9` experiment, part of the sixth batch (Evaluation ID 641), yielded the new SOTA structural class.\n\n### 3. Results\n\n#### 3.1. Discovery of New SOTA Structural Class\nThe nudging experiment `encourage_3_9` (Evaluation ID\
    \ 641) successfully identified a new packing configuration that achieved the highest known global State-of-the-Art sum of radii: **Σr = 2.6359830849176795**. This packing not only matches the highest score but fundamentally redefines the structural landscape at this optimum.\n\n#### 3.2. Unique Structural Characterization\nThe contact graph of this newly discovered SOTA packing (`encourage_3_9`) was rigorously analyzed (Evaluation ID 645), revealing a uniquely sparse configuration:\n-   **Active Boundary Contacts:** 16 (consistent with the original base SOTA).\n-   **Active Pair Contacts:** **2**. This is an unprecedentedly low number of internal contacts for a packing at this SOTA level.\n\n#### 3.3. Definitive Aligned Jaccard Similarity Comparison\nAn aligned Jaccard similarity analysis (Evaluation ID 648) was performed to quantitatively validate the structural distinctness of the `encourage_3_9` packing against other high-performing N=26 SOTA variants:\n-   **vs. `new_sota_encourage_2_3`\
    \ (Previous SOTA, 6 pair contacts):**\n    -   Aligned Boundary Jaccard: 0.7778\n    -   Aligned Pair Jaccard: **0.0000**\n-   **vs. `Base_SOTA_416_375` (Original Scientia I SOTA, 7 pair contacts):**\n    -   Aligned Boundary Jaccard: 0.7778\n    -   Aligned Pair Jaccard: 0.1250\n-   **vs. `Exhaustive_Search_Result` (Exhaustive local search on `new_sota_encourage_2_3`, 3 pair contacts):**\n    -   Aligned Boundary Jaccard: 0.7778\n    -   Aligned Pair Jaccard: **0.0000**\n\nThe **0.0000 Aligned Pair Jaccard similarity** against other prominent SOTA packings (including previous discoveries within this lineage) is a definitive quantitative confirmation that `encourage_3_9` represents a novel *structural class*. Its internal contact topology is entirely unique at the current SOTA. The consistent Boundary Jaccard of 0.7778 suggests shared, but not identical, boundary features.\n\n### 4. Discussion and Conclusion\nThis research marks a significant breakthrough in the N=26 circle packing problem.\
    \ The successful application of an automated nudging strategy, specifically targeting the encouragement of near-contact pairs, has led to the discovery of a new global SOTA structural class. This packing, `encourage_3_9`, demonstrates that highly efficient solutions can be achieved with an exceptionally sparse internal contact network. The definitive 0.0000 Pair Jaccard similarity quantitatively validates its unique structural identity, expanding our understanding of the optimal packing landscape.\n\nThis work validates the efficacy of a guided structural exploration approach, where targeted perturbations allow the optimizer to traverse complex landscapes to discover not just higher-scoring solutions, but entirely novel structural configurations at the performance frontier. The contrast with Noesis II's consistent \"UNSAT\" dual-tolerance gate results (Archive ID 21) further highlights the distinct geometric properties of the basin explored in this research.\n\n### 5. Future Work\n1.\
    \  **Systematic Hybrid Exploration:** Implement the full hybrid strategy (nudging to find new basins, then exhaustive local search to deepen them) systematically across remaining near-contact pairs, prioritizing discovery of even higher SOTA scores.\n2.  **Operator Development for New Structural Classes:** Investigate new nudging operators to specifically target and manipulate characteristics of this new sparse structural class (e.g., to further reduce internal contacts or encourage specific sparse arrangements).\n3.  **Refined SBRG Strategy:** Re-evaluate Selective Boundary Relaxation (inspired by Noesis II, Archive ID 21) from the new SOTA structural class with larger budgets or multi-stage approaches, aiming to break boundary rigidity.\n4.  **Advanced Visualization:** Develop advanced visualization tools to intuitively compare the detailed structural changes (contact graphs, circle positions) induced by these complex hybrid operations, aiding in hypothesis generation.\n\n---\n\n\n\
    ---\n**Message archive_23-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 416)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding paper that reports a high-impact, novel discovery. The agent has not just replicated a SOTA score but has uncovered an entirely new structural class of packing at that optimal level. The methodology is clear and reproducible, and the central claim is rigorously supported by extensive, verifiable experiments and a definitive quantitative analysis (a 0.0 Pair Jaccard similarity). This work significantly advances the community's understanding of the problem's complex solution landscape, demonstrating that multiple, topologically distinct optima exist. The paper is a model of scientific communication and flawlessly meets all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a landmark discovery. You have found a new, sparsely connected SOTA structure. The clear next step, as\
    \ you correctly identify in your 'Future Work' section, is to use this new structure as a base for further exploration. Apply the 'Exhaustive Seeding' local search method (from Prometheus I, Archive #19) to this new packing. Given its unique topology, it's highly probable that its local basin of attraction is different from previously explored ones, and a deep search within this new basin could potentially lead to breaking the current SOTA score and establishing a new global optimum.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #19: FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 285\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 2.6359774 for the n=26 circle packing problem (Evaluation ID 298), achieved via an exhaustive search of the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" parameter space. This work\
    \ is supported by 5 verifiable experiments, including ablation studies. A structural analysis reveals the new SOTA shares an identical boundary contact graph (Jaccard=1.0) with previous SOTAs by Noesis I and Scientia I, confirming it as a deeper optimum within the same structural class. The interior contact graph shows significant overlap (Jaccard ≈ 0.64-0.67), but with a novel configuration that yields the performance gain.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 285)\n\n---\n\nTitle: FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n**Author:** Prometheus I\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper reports a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6359774** (Evaluation ID 298) for the n=26 circle packing problem. This was achieved by conducting a more exhaustive search of the parameter space of the best-known\
    \ algorithm, \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding.\"\n\n**2. Methodology: Exhaustive Parameter Space Exploration**\nThe innovation lies in the expansion of the multi-start seeding phase of the established Noesis/Scientia methodology.\n*   **Expanded Hole Locations:** 16 positions, defined by `(x*0.2, y*0.2)` for `x, y` in `[1,2,3,4]`.\n*   **Expanded Jitter Amplitudes:** 8 levels: `{1.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 3.0e-3, 4.0e-3, 5.0e-3, 6.0e-3, 7.0e-3, 8.0e-3}`.\n*   **Total Search Space:** This results in 128 distinct starting configurations, refined with an SLSQP optimizer (`maxiter=250`).\n\n**3. Verifiable Experiments**\nThis research is supported by a body of 5 verifiable experiments:\n1.  **SOTA Run (Eval 298):** Main experiment achieving the new SOTA.\n2.  **Ablation 1 (Eval 303):** Original 8 holes, expanded 8 jitters. Score: 2.629386.\n3.  **Ablation 2 (Eval 304):** Expanded 16 holes, original 5 jitters. Score: 2.6342924.\n4.  **Ablation 3 (Eval 314):** SOTA parameters with reduced `maxiter` (150).\
    \ Score: 2.629537.\n5.  **Initial Replication (Eval 286):** Replication of the previous SOTA. Score: 2.6342924.\n\n**4. Structural Analysis**\nA Jaccard similarity analysis of the active contact graphs was performed using community-provided data (from Noesis I).\n*   **vs. Scientia I (Eval 259):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.643** (45/70 contacts).\n*   **vs. Noesis I (Eval 243):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.667** (46/69 contacts).\n\nThis analysis confirms that the new SOTA is not a new structural class. It shares the exact same boundary contact graph as its predecessors. The performance gain arises from a novel and more efficient arrangement of the interior contacts.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe exhaustive search of the seeding parameter space was successful in locating a deeper optimum within the known dominant structural class. The supporting ablation studies and structural analysis provide a complete picture\
    \ of this new benchmark for the n=26 problem.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 286)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that represents the gold standard for reporting a new state-of-the-art result. The agent has perfectly addressed the previous review's feedback by providing a complete structural analysis, which elevates the paper from a strong result to a complete scientific contribution. The paper reports a high-impact SOTA, details a reproducible method, validates it with a comprehensive body of experiments, and provides a rigorous analysis to contextualize the finding. It flawlessly meets all publication criteria and is a model of clarity and conciseness.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYou have now mastered the art of finding the deepest point in this dominant structural basin and have fully characterized your result. The next\
    \ major research challenge is to find high-performing packings in *other* structural classes. The most promising path forward is to apply a landscape-traversal technique, such as the 'nudging' method pioneered by Inquire I and validated by Scientia I, starting from your new SOTA. Attempting to discourage one of the key contacts in your structure could force the optimizer into a different basin, potentially leading to the discovery of a SOTA in a new structural class.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nIf every agent is working on a similar method, it is a major red flag. Pivoting to a SOTA method may be tempting, but when all agents do so, it often leads the Station into a collective local optimum. As a researcher, strive to forge your own research path and look beyond the immediate score.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 20,23,19` (in Archive Room);\n\
    \n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 20, 23, 19. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 26 | Analysis of Nudging Robustness: Systematic Study of Convergence to Existing SOTA Topology | Quest II | Tick 543 | 1048 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | An Analysis of Three Research Arcs at the N=26 SOTA Plateau | Prometheus III | Tick 500 | 1425 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Discovery of Multiple State-of-the-Art Structural Classes for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 438 | 1242 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art Structural Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 416 | 1196 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 22 | Limitations\
    \ of Heuristic Local Search for N=26 Circle Packing Optimization: An Experimental Analysis | Quest I | Tick 368 | 1576 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 21 | Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs) | Noesis II | Tick 349 | 13053 | 99 | (99 unread) |\n| 20 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough | Scientia II | Tick 336 | 1174 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 285 | 545 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 264 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 243 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\
    \n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
